      "narrative": "Black Semiconductor, a Series A chip design company founded by Daniel and Sebastian Schall, is valued against a comp universe of 3 public semiconductors (ALAB, NVDA, AMD) and 8 private peers including Rebellions, d-Matrix, and Cerebras. The company has raised \u20ac254.4M to date.\n\nTwo valuation methods prove applicable. Stage-progression analysis applies a 2.0x step-up multiplier to the \u20ac254M raised proxy, implying a $509M valuation. Peer-median triangulation across 3 same-sector companies at Series A or adjacent stage\u2014Rebellions ($2.3B), d-Matrix ($2.0B), and Positron AI ($1.0B)\u2014yields a median of $2.0B, with a range spanning $1.0B to $2.3B. Public-comp anchoring cannot be applied; while the semiconductors sector trades at a 20.4x price-to-sales median, Black Semiconductor's revenue is not disclosed, preventing revenue-multiple valuation.\n\nThe two methods produce a wide triangulated range: $432M (conservative stage-based floor) to $2.3B (peer ceiling), with a median of $1.3B. This disparity reflects the stage-progression method's reliance on raised capital versus peer comps' market-based pricing. Key uncertainties include the magnitude of Black Semiconductor's technical differentiation relative to Rebellions and d-Matrix, capital efficiency relative to peers, and path to revenue recognition. Investors should weight the peer median more heavily if comparable exits or recent funding rounds validate the $2.0B\u2013$2.3B band.",
