      "narrative": "Telexistence, a Series B robotics and manufacturing company founded by Jin Tomioka and Ken Tomoyose, has raised $170M+ to date. The valuation analysis draws from a peer set of three same-stage, same-sector private companies (Generalist AI at $2.0B, Bedrock Robotics at $1.8B, and Dexterity at $1.6B), supplemented by a broader universe of eight private robotics firms and one public comparable.\n\nTwo valuation methods were applied. Stage-progression analysis uses the $170M total raised as a proxy baseline and applies a 1.9x multiplier typical for Series B advancement, yielding $323M. Peer-median analysis benchmarks Telexistence directly against the three identified comparables, which trade at a median valuation of $1.8B with a range of $1.6B to $2.0B. This method reflects current market pricing for robotics companies at similar development stages.\n\nTriangulation of both methods produces a valuation range of $275M (low) to $2.0B (high), with a median of $1.0B. The wide spread reflects tension between stage-based cost-plus reasoning and peer multiples, which suggest significantly higher valuations. Key uncertainties include revenue scale and growth trajectory relative to peers, customer concentration risk, and whether Telexistence's technology roadmap justifies peer multiples. Additional clarity on near-term financing activity and revenue run rate would narrow this range materially.",
