      "narrative": "Distalmotion, a Series G robotics and manufacturing company founded by Michael Friedrich, has raised $150M+ to date. The valuation universe comprises 9 private peers (including Divergent, Exotec, and Locus Robotics) and 1 public comparable, though none yield a sufficiently dense peer set for robust median-based triangulation at the Series G stage.\n\nGiven data constraints, stage-progression analysis provides the primary valuation anchor. Using total raised ($150M) as a proxy base value and applying a typical 1.25x step-up multiplier for Series G advancement yields an implied valuation of $188M. This method assumes capital deployment has been reasonably efficient and that subsequent-stage entry multiples hold for this cohort.\n\nThe analysis supports a valuation range of $159M (low) to $216M (median), with $188M as the central point estimate. The range reflects standard stage-progression variance rather than triangulation across multiple methods. Key uncertainties include the absence of contemporaneous Series G robotics comps at similar valuation tiers, which limits confidence in the multiplier assumption, and limited visibility into Distalmotion's unit economics, revenue trajectory, or capital deployment efficiency relative to peers. A narrower range would require additional peer data or portfolio company financials at comparable stages.",
