      "narrative": "Oxide Computer Company is a Series C semiconductor company founded by Steve Tuck and Bryan Cantrill, having raised $389M to date. Valuation analysis draws on a comp universe of 3 public semiconductor players (ALAB, NVDA, AMD) and 8 private peers in chips and semiconductors, including stage-adjacent companies like Tenstorrent ($2.7B, Series D), Nearfield Instruments ($1.6B, Series D), Positron AI ($1.0B, Series B), and DEEPX ($529M, Series C).\n\nTwo quantitative methods triangulate a valuation range. Stage-progression methodology applies a 1.7x step-up multiplier to the $389M capital raised, implying $661M valuation. Peer-median benchmarking across four same-sector and adjacent-stage comparables yields a $1.3B median, with a range spanning $529M to $2.7B. Public comp anchoring using a 20.4x price-to-sales median across the three public tickers cannot be applied due to absent revenue disclosure typical of private companies.\n\nSynthesizing these two applicable methods produces a triangulated range of $562M (low) to $1.5B (high), with a median of $981M. The wide dispersion reflects material uncertainty around Oxide's relative position within the semiconductor stack and its capital efficiency relative to both earlier-stage and more mature funded peers. Stage-progression and peer medians diverge notably, suggesting valuation sensitivity to assumed growth trajectory and comparability weightings.",
