      "narrative": "SF Compute is a Series A semiconductor company founded by Evan Conrad and Alex Gajewski that has raised $52M to date. The valuation analysis draws on a comp universe of 3 public semiconductor peers (ALAB, NVDA, AMD) and 8 private frontier-chip companies including Rebellions, d-Matrix, and Cerebras at similar or adjacent stages.\n\nTwo triangulation methods prove applicable. Stage-progression analysis uses the $52M total raised as a valuation proxy and applies a 2.0x multiplier typical of Series A-to-next-stage advancement, implying $104M. Peer-median anchoring references three same-sector companies at Series A or B stage\u2014Rebellions ($2.3B), d-Matrix ($2.0B), and Positron AI ($1.0B)\u2014yielding a median valuation of $2.0B. Public-comp anchoring via P/S multiple cannot be applied because SF Compute's revenue is not on file. The sector median P/S across the three public comparables is 20.4x, but remains unusable without target-level revenue disclosure.\n\nThe triangulated analysis supports a valuation range of $88M (low) to $2.3B (high), with a median of $1.1B. The wide dispersion reflects material uncertainty: stage-progression and peer-median methods diverge substantially, suggesting either that SF Compute is early-stage relative to its peer set or that the private comp universe exhibits high variance in valuation discipline. Key unknowns include SF Compute's revenue trajectory, capital efficiency relative to peers, and technology differentiation within the crowded frontier-chip landscape.",
